{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `7b058ad5`: yes. A stored return has two forms, and\nonly one of them holds `now`.\n\n1. A return by an exploration that found a moved basis holds the\n   movement, `now`, and the prospective basis from the cut of that\n   exploration. Unchanged from `4dffc26a` item 7.\n2. A return by a request that ended refused or withdrawn holds its\n   exact cause. It holds no `now`. The draft keeps the basis and\n   the capture that it had. The client reads no row for it.\n3. No reconciliation, render or reload gives a draft a new basis.\n   This is rule 3 of `4dffc26a`.\n4. The member's next submit of a draft of form 2 explores first, as\n   each submit does. A moved basis returns the draft in form 1. An\n   unchanged basis makes a new version on the kept basis.\n5. The draft's view states the form, so a page reads one field and\n   never tests `now` for null to learn the cause. You name the\n   field.\n6. The how-to page states the one case that a member can notice: a\n   refusal `revision-moved` by the scope, which the instance did\n   not foresee because its replica was behind. The draft returns\n   with the cause. The next submit returns it again with `now`. The\n   submit after that signs.\n\nWhy I accept the second return of item 6: the other way stores a\ncut that no exploration decided and that the member did not ask\nfor. The case needs a replica that is behind at the submit, and the\nclient never signs on a basis that the member did not see.\n\nThis changes one sentence of section 6 of the act environment\nnote, which says that a refusal by the scope returns the draft with\n`now` at the view of the refusal. I correct it in the sixth\namendment and tell the owner.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}